## registering

cpy .1, 100
wln *1 # outputs 100

## printing

wln VALUE # with newline
w VALUE # without newline

## captures

.1 # capture value into register
_ # discard captured value
%0 # capture into argument
out # capture into function result
# wherever you see <capture>, these can be used there

## reading

rei <capture> # integer
rech <capture> # character (0-255)
rek <capture> # key (0-255), also the expressional output of this is 1 if the pressed key was a modifier otherwise 0.
# expressional output i mean what it produces as an expression, not what it writes into <capture>

## body

{ wln "hello" } # code body
wln "hello" # single-expression body

# the last expression in a code body is the result value, by last i mean the last expression that gets executed
# not the syntactic last expression.
# for example:
# here either hello or bye are the last expressions
wln {
    if A {
        "hello"
    } else {
        "bye"
    }
}


## loops

while CONDITION BODY

for <capture> = <expr> to <expr> step <expr> BODY # step is optional

## some more expressions

-<factor> # negate
+=<factor> # increase the factor and produce
-=<factor> # decrease the factor and produce
*<capture> # try to dereference a capture and get its value, you can also use an integer which is just seen as a register

## functions




func IDENTIFIER : ARGCOUNT BODY # you can omit the ": ARGCOUNT" part to get no-arg func (purely syntax-sugar)

# calling a function:
IDENTIFIER(ARGS): <capture>

# <capture> is the return value register, the function can access this capture via the 'out' keyword and dereference it via '*out'
# you can obviously use _ to ignore the value
# you can omit '(ARGS)' to provide no args.

# also you can store the address of a function in a register:
cpy .1, IDENTIFIER

# and then use it later
(*1)(ARGS): <capture>

# this isnt special to registers, a symbol access simply returns its address so you can also pass function addresses as args!

# you can access a function's parameters via the modulo operator:
wln %0 # prints the first parameter




## conditionals

eq A, B # produces 1 if true, 0 if false
gt A, B
lt A, B

## logicals

and A, B
or A, B
xor A, B
not X # 1 if false, 0 if true

## if statements
if <expr> BODY
elseif <expr2> BODY
elseif <expr3> BODY
...
else BODY



## arrays

# you can reserve an array and store it's address
cpy .1, [:10] # reserve space for 10 elements

# or you can use an array literal
cpy .1, [1, 20, 300, 4000] # reserve array with 4 elements, [1, 20, 300, 4000]

# you can access the value of an array's field like this
# ADDRESS->INDEX
# for example:
*1->0 # gets the first element

# you can also assign elements
# ADDRESS->INDEX = VALUE
# for example:
*1->0 = 10

# you can quickly print the representation of an array
# wln ~ADDRESS
wln ~*1


## the '~' operator

this operator simply takes an address and returns the value that the address refers to.
we use it to print arrays for example; arrays simply print a simple representation of themselves when printed.


## modules

# you can import a module like this
fetch "path/to/module.zy"

# modules are just zyte files executed at runtime.
# they share the same register file and definitions and address space as the current module
# meaning along with the importer being able to use stuff from the importee
# the importee can also use stuff that the importee "thinks" will be defined in the importer when its imported.
# the "examples/engine_importable.zy" file is an example of this
# it calls a tick function that doesn't exist in the file itself, instead the importer is expected to define it and then call the start function.


## standard library functions

# length function
len(addr_of_array): .1 # the length gets written into reg 1



# enjoy!